% ============================================================================
% quadrilateral-alt-name — for the quadrilateral where the SAME source
% names an alternate word, the alternate name (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOMETRY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `quadrilateral-types.adj`
% (`quadrilateral_property(shape, property)`, ONE defining property per
% quadrilateral: square, rectangle, rhombus, parallelogram, trapezoid).
% That table's `property` column has room for only the defining PROPERTY;
% the SAME already-quoted MathWorld rhombus sentence also states an
% ALTERNATE NAME for it that never made it into a row:
%
%   rhombus → equilateral_parallelogram
%     "A rhombus is a quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides
%      parallel and all sides the same length, i.e., an equilateral
%      parallelogram."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_alt_name(rhombus, $AltName)      % equilateral_parallelogram
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like
% `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own `quadrilateral_property`): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `quadrilateral_alt_name(shape, alt_name)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the alternate name WITH its source, on the
% CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on square, rectangle,
% parallelogram, and trapezoid, whose own already-cited spans in
% `quadrilateral-types.adj` state no alternate name to decode here.
%
% Only ONE of `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s five quadrilaterals has a
% genuinely distinct alternate name in the ALREADY-cited spans; this table
% is deliberately narrow rather than inventing a name the source does not
% state. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind an alternate name and recall
% which quadrilateral it names:
%
%     ? quadrilateral_alt_name($Shape, equilateral_parallelogram)   % rhombus
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME rhombus sentence already
% quoted inside `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own provenance block, from the
% SAME MathWorld "Rhombus" page that table already cites — no new
% WebFetch, only a different schema decoding a clause of an
% already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope
% (per `quadrilateral-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold that full sentence — the single span that fixes this
% table's only row. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `quadrilateral-types.adj` already carries (Wolfram MathWorld, a primary
% mathematics reference).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table quadrilateral_alt_name {
    columns shape, alt_name

    row (rhombus, equilateral_parallelogram)

    source "A rhombus is a quadrilateral with both pairs of opposite sides parallel and all sides the same length, i.e., an equilateral parallelogram."
    locator "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Rhombus.html"
    trust authoritative
}
